                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 extra kit</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Buy all your kits from the same production batch if you can, and box them together before you start. Color can shift slightly between batches, and the transition will show along the seam where one kit ends and the next begins. Most home centers will take back an unopened extra kit, so buying one more than you need is cheap insurance.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan a clear topcoat, count it separately; most water-based base kits do not include enough clear for a full second layer. High-solids direct-sold kits are often sold by garage size with the topcoat included, so read exactly what is in each box. Our <a href="/epoxy-garage-floor-cost/">epoxy garage floor cost guide</a> breaks down what a full two-car job costs once tools, crack filler and extras are added.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Slab Prep Decides Whether Any Kit Lasts
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy bonds mechanically. It needs a clean surface with a texture, called a profile, that it can lock into. The industry describes profile with a scale from the International Concrete Repair Institute; for garage coatings the usual target is roughly the feel of 120-grit sandpaper. Power-troweled garage slabs are often smooth and dense, which is why a coating can look perfect for a month and then lift off in sheets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test for moisture first</h3>

          <p>
            Tape an 18-by-18-inch square of clear plastic sheeting to the bare floor, sealing every edge, and leave it for 24 hours. Condensation under the plastic or darkened concrete means vapor is moving up through the slab, and that pressure will push a coating off. Hold off on any film coating until that is solved, or choose a vapor-tolerant primer that the coating maker approves for the conditions you have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Degrease, then profile</h3>

          <p>
            Remove every oil and fluid stain before you etch. Acid will not cut oil, and epoxy will not stick to it. A concrete degreaser and stiff brush handle fresh drips; deep, old stains may need a poultice. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/">getting oil out of concrete</a> walks through it. Then open the surface. Citric or acid etch works on many older, porous slabs. A rented diamond grinder with a vacuum attachment is far more reliable on smooth or previously sealed floors, and it is the step most often skipped by people whose floors peel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your work with a water drop test. Sprinkle a few drops on the prepared concrete. If they soak in within a minute or so, the surface is open enough. If they bead up, something is still sealing the slab: a curing compound, old sealer, or residue. Keep working that area. The full sequence, including crack repair, is laid out step by step in our guide to <a href="/how-to-apply-garage-floor-epoxy/">applying garage floor epoxy</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="conditions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Temperature, Humidity and Pot Life
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most garage epoxies list an application range of roughly 50-90 degrees Fahrenheit, and that range applies to the concrete, not just the air. Slabs stay cold long after the air warms up, especially in spring. An infrared thermometer costs less than one kit and removes the guesswork. Aim for a slab in the 60s or low 70s and stable weather for the next day or two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dew point matters as much as temperature. If the slab is within about 5 degrees of the dew point, moisture condenses on the surface and can leave a cloudy, greasy film called amine blush on some epoxies, or simply weaken the bond. Many free weather apps show dew point; compare it with your slab reading before you mix. Morning coats on a cold slab followed by a humid afternoon are a common recipe for trouble.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical label numbers to check</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50-90°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common application range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Minimum slab margin above dew point</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">24 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usual wait for foot traffic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Usual wait before parking</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Pot life is the time a mixed batch stays workable in the bucket. Heat shortens it, and so does leaving the mixture in a deep pail where the reaction builds on itself. Pour the mixed material into a roller tray or onto the floor promptly, and mix only what you can spread in the time the label allows. When a batch starts to thicken or warm in the pail, stop using it. Rolling partially gelled epoxy leaves ridges that never level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cure times on labels assume moderate temperature. In a cool garage they stretch, sometimes by days. Foot traffic is commonly allowed after about a day and vehicles after three to seven days, but follow your product and add time if the garage is chilly. Driving on an under-cured floor is one of the most reliable ways to get permanent tire marks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flakes-topcoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Flakes and Clear Topcoats: Worth the Extra Step?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Decorative vinyl flakes are broadcast by hand into the wet base coat. They do three useful jobs. They hide dust, small stains and roller marks. They break up the surface so it is less slippery. And on a full-broadcast system, where flakes cover the floor completely and the excess is scraped off, they add body that helps hide an imperfect slab. A light sprinkle is mostly cosmetic; heavier coverage changes how the floor looks and feels.
          </p>

          <p>
            Throw flakes up and out so they rain down evenly instead of landing in clumps. Work within the window the label gives for broadcasting, usually while the base is still wet enough to grab them. Practice on a piece of cardboard first. Most kits include enough flakes for a light-to-medium sprinkle; for heavier coverage you will need to buy more separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            A clear topcoat locks the flakes in, adds gloss, and gives the floor a sacrificial wear layer. Water-based base kits benefit the most, because their film is thin. If any sunlight reaches the floor through an open door or windows, choose a UV-stable topcoat; standard epoxy yellows in sunlight over time. Polyaspartic and urethane clears hold their color well, while plain epoxy clears are cheaper but can amber.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gloss has a downside: wet, smooth coatings get slick. Ask for, or buy separately, an anti-slip additive to mix into the final coat. It is a fine grit that adds traction without making the floor hard to mop. On a floor that sees rain and snow tracked in by tires, it is not optional.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hot-tire" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Hot-Tire Pickup and How to Avoid It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hot-tire pickup is the coating lifting off the floor and sticking to your tires. It happens because warm rubber softens the coating slightly and grips it, and as the tire cools and contracts it pulls. If the bond between coating and concrete is weaker than the bond between coating and tire, the coating leaves with the car. Owners commonly report it in the first months after an install, usually in the exact spot where they park.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three things drive it, in this order: weak surface prep, a soft coating, and an under-cured film. Mechanical grinding produces a stronger bond than acid etching on most slabs. Two-part coatings resist it better than one-part paints, and high-solids or hybrid products better than thin water-based films. And waiting the full cure time before parking makes a measurable difference, especially when the garage is cool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some tires are worse than others. Performance summer tires and fresh rubber run softer and warmer. If you drive a performance car or commute on the highway and pull straight in, factor that into your choice and consider a high-solids system with a tough topcoat.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already have peeling under the tires, patching rarely fixes it for long because the bond around the failed spot is usually also weak. The durable fix is grinding off the affected lane and recoating. For floors where tire marks are the main worry, interlocking tiles avoid the problem entirely; see our ranking of <a href="/best-garage-floor-tiles/">garage floor tiles</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrong-choice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When Epoxy Is the Wrong Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Epoxy is a film, and films need a dry, sound, clean base. If your slab fails the plastic-sheet moisture test, has efflorescence (white, chalky deposits) seeping through, or sits on a site with poor drainage, a film coating is likely to blister or peel no matter how well it is applied. Vapor-tolerant primers exist, but they add cost and still need the moisture level checked against the maker's limit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Badly damaged concrete is another red flag. Wide structural cracks that move with the seasons, heavy spalling from road salt, or a slab that is heaving will crack any rigid coating above them. Fix the slab first or choose a floating floor. Interlocking tiles sit on top without bonding and tolerate imperfect concrete, which is why they are popular in older garages.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rental properties and homes you plan to sell soon may also be better served by something simpler. A penetrating densifier or a clear sealer from our list of <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealers</a> keeps dust down and helps stains wipe off, with almost no risk of a peeling surface that a buyer sees on day one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider your own time. A proper DIY epoxy job takes most of two weekends with prep, and the garage is out of service for days afterward. If that is not realistic, a professional installer or a product with a faster return to service is worth the cost. Weigh it honestly before buying the first box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Living With an Epoxy Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            The upside of a coated floor is how easy it is to keep clean. Sweep or use a dust mop weekly to remove grit, because grit under tires and shoes is what slowly dulls the surface. Mop with warm water and a mild, pH-neutral cleaner. Avoid citrus or vinegar cleaners in strong concentrations, and skip soap-based products that leave a slippery film.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wipe up spills quickly. Cured epoxy handles motor oil and antifreeze well, but brake fluid, some solvents and battery acid can soften or stain it if left for hours. Keep a roll of shop towels and a bottle of cleaner near the workbench. Put a mat under the car in winter to catch salty slush, and squeegee standing water toward the door or drain.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protect against point loads. Set plywood pads under jack stands, use furniture glides under heavy cabinets, and lay a mat where you kneel and drop tools. Dragging a steel toolbox across the floor will scratch any coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            After a few years, a light scuff-sand and a fresh clear topcoat can restore gloss and add life, provided the base coat is still well bonded. That maintenance coat is far cheaper than a full redo. A pressure washer on a low setting with a wide nozzle can help deep-clean a garage before recoating; our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer guide</a> lists models suited to that job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a record of what went down: product name, color, batch number and the date. When a section needs repair years later, that note tells you exactly what to buy and which topcoats are compatible. Store a small amount of leftover flakes too, so a patched area blends in rather than standing out as a bare gray square in the middle of a speckled floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the etch packet on a smooth slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The citric etch included in many kits is mild. On a dense, power-troweled garage floor it may barely open the surface. If water still beads after etching, the coating will not grip. Rent a diamond grinder with a vacuum shroud for a day; it costs less than redoing a peeled floor and gives a far more consistent profile.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the moisture test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vapor pushing up through the slab is invisible until the coating blisters. A 24-hour plastic-sheet test costs almost nothing. If the concrete darkens or water collects under the plastic, stop and address the moisture or choose a vapor-tolerant primer approved by the coating maker before applying any film coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing the whole kit at once in hot weather</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mixed epoxy generates heat, and a full pail in a warm garage can gel well before the label's pot life. Mix in partial batches if the system allows, pour onto the floor or into a tray promptly, and plan the floor so you can keep a wet edge without rushing the last section.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying exactly enough coating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Porous, freshly profiled concrete absorbs far more on the first coat than the label assumes. Running short mid-floor leaves a visible seam where a new batch meets a coat that is already setting. Buy at least one extra kit, keep the receipt, and return it unopened if you do not need it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking before the coating has cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A floor can feel hard to the touch long before it is fully cured, especially in a cool garage. Warm tires on an under-cured film are a classic cause of hot-tire marks. Wait the full time on the label, add a few days when the slab is cold, and keep the car out until then.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What service life should I expect from an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends mostly on film thickness and prep. A water-based kit on a well-prepared slab commonly lasts around three to five years in a garage with daily parking before it needs a refresh. High-solids and 100% solids systems, applied over a ground floor with a topcoat, often last a decade or more. Poor prep can shorten either to months.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a water-based kit like EpoxyShield good enough for a two-car garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For many households, yes, as long as the slab is sound, dry and properly profiled. It gives a clean look and easy cleanup. Where it falls short is heavy wear: creepers, jacks, studded tires and constant traffic will thin it faster than a high-solids system. Plan on a topcoat or refresh after a few years.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I have to grind the floor, or is acid etching enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Etching can work on older, porous slabs that absorb water quickly. On smooth, dense or previously sealed concrete, it often fails to create enough texture. Grinding is the more reliable method and is what most professional installers use. If water beads up on your floor after cleaning, plan on grinding rather than etching.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I apply epoxy over an old painted or coated floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the old coating is the same type, well bonded and properly scuff-sanded, and the new product's label allows it. Peeling or flaking paint must come off first, usually by grinding. Applying a two-part epoxy over a weak one-part paint simply bonds the new coat to a layer that will fail. When in doubt, grind back to bare concrete.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How soon can I drive on a new epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most two-part garage kits list roughly three to seven days before vehicle traffic at moderate temperatures, and polycuramine or polyaspartic systems can be shorter. Cool slabs slow curing, so add time in spring and fall. Foot traffic is usually allowed after about a day. Always follow the specific label rather than a general rule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my epoxy floor so slippery when wet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A smooth, glossy coating has little texture, so water and slush make it slick. The fix is an anti-slip additive, a fine grit mixed into the topcoat or final coat, or a heavier flake broadcast that leaves texture. If the floor is already finished, a new topcoat with additive can add grip without starting over.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth hiring a pro instead of buying a kit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A professional install costs several times more but usually includes grinding, crack repair and a thick multi-layer system, often polyaspartic, with a warranty. If your slab has problems, you lack time for proper prep, or you want a decade-plus floor, a pro is often worth it. For a sound slab and a modest budget, a DIY kit makes sense.
              </p>
            </div>

