          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most people coating a sound one- or two-car slab, Rust-Oleum EpoxyShield is the sensible place to start, as long as you grind or thoroughly etch the floor and buy an extra kit. If you park daily, work on cars, or want the garage back quickly, RockSolid Polycuramine is worth the extra money. If you are already renting a grinder and want a floor closer to a professional job, a thick-build system from ArmorPoxy or Epoxy-Coat gives you far more film per coat.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whichever kit you pick, the slab decides the outcome. Test for moisture, remove every trace of oil, create a real profile, watch the dew point, and wait out the full cure before you park. Do those five things and even a budget kit will look good for years; skip them and the most expensive bucket on the shelf will still peel.
